WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.134
An AI homelab produces and consumes many kinds of data: prompts, outputs, datasets, documents, generated files, embeddings, model artifacts, logs, screenshots, notebooks, and configuration.

00:00:13.134 --> 00:00:17.512
Some of that data may be sensitive even if the lab is personal.

00:00:17.512 --> 00:00:24.183
Prompts can include names, internal notes, source code, personal records, or client information.

00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:27.588
Outputs can repeat or transform that information.

00:00:27.588 --> 00:00:31.479
Logs can capture paths, tokens, errors, and identifiers.

00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:33.425
Organize data intentionally.

00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:39.957
Separate personal data, client data, experiments, public test data, and stable project assets.

00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:46.350
Use clear folders for models, datasets, outputs, temporary processing, and archived results.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.686
This makes backup and deletion decisions easier.

00:00:49.686 --> 00:01:01.638
It also reduces the chance that a public demo, shared screenshot, cloud sync folder, or repository commit accidentally includes information that should have stayed private.

00:01:01.638 --> 00:01:13.799
Backups should focus on the assets that are hard to recreate: configuration, notes, prompts you need to preserve, curated datasets, application code, and workflow definitions.

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:21.443
Large model files can often be downloaded again, while carefully prepared datasets or notebooks may be unique.

00:01:21.443 --> 00:01:26.655
Encryption at rest may be appropriate when sensitive information is stored.

00:01:26.655 --> 00:01:29.018
Retention and deletion matter too.

00:01:29.018 --> 00:01:35.272
Keeping every experiment forever can turn a learning lab into a growing privacy liability.

00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:38.677
Treat the homelab as a data-handling environment.

00:01:38.677 --> 00:01:49.934
Decide what data is allowed, where it can be stored, whether cloud sync is acceptable, who can access shared folders, and how outputs are reviewed before sharing.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:54.521
Avoid putting secrets in prompts, logs, notebooks, or screenshots.

00:01:54.521 --> 00:02:04.041
Local AI can improve privacy compared with sending everything to a cloud service, but only if the local environment is managed carefully.
